le {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} " 9 August 2024 — 15:00"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/2165"} "Paris Expo Porte de Versailles, Paris, France"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/2013218"} "12 from 12 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Total of best lifts in snatch and clean &amp; jerk determined placement. Ties broken by best result's attempt number – the athlete who achieved the result earliest according to the Calling Order."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "For the Paris Games, the men’s light-heavyweight class had a slight change from Tokyo, with the weight limit moving up from 81 kg to 89 kg."] "\n\n" [:p {} "China’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/121984"} "Lu Xiaojun"] ", who won gold in Tokyo, was not in Paris to defend his title, having previously become the oldest Olympian to win a weightlifting gold at 37 years and 4 days. In December 2022, Lu had also been provisionally suspended following an out-of-competition positive test for EPO. At the same time, " [:a {:href "/athletes/142244"} "Zacarías Bonnat"] " (DOM), who had won silver in Tokyo, was also provisionally suspended following a positive test for a banned substance. This meant that the Italian lifter " [:a {:href "/athletes/144234"} "Antonino Pizzolato"] ", who had won bronze in Tokyo, was the only medal winner from the previous Games to compete in Paris."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Favorites for medals included the world record holder in the snatch " [:a {:href "/athletes/155550"} "Yeison López"] " (COL), the world record holder in the clean &amp; jerk " [:a {:href "/athletes/155545"} "Karlos Nasar"] " (BUL), and the reigning World Champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/155563"} "Mir Mostafa Javadi"] " (IRI)."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Following the snatch, both López and Nasar had lifted 180 kg, with López leading due to lighter bodyweight. They were closely followed by " [:a {:href "/athletes/1700439"} "Marin Robu"] " (MDA) and Pizzolato. Nasar then lifted 224 kg in the clean &amp; jerk, with López only managing 210 kg and finishing behind Pizzolato, who lifted 212 kg on his third and final attempt. Nasar won gold with a world record lift of 404 kg, with López taking silver, and Pizzolato winning his second consecutive bronze."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Kilograms"] [:th {} "Bodyweight"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/155545"} "Karlos Nasar"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/BUL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/BUL.png"}] "BUL"]] [:td {} "404"] [:td {} "88.40"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "World Record"} "WR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/155550"} "Yeison López"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/COL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/COL.png"}] "COL"]] [:td {} "390"] [:td {} "88.20"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/144234"} "Antonino Pizzolato"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ITA.png"}] "ITA"]] [:td {} "384"] [:td {} "89.00"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/1700439"} "Marin Robu"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/MDA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazona